<ExplainerLayout title="KL Divergence" breadcrumb="KL Divergence" section="information-theory">

<Hero title="KL Divergence" subtitle="A directed measure of the extra cost of using one distribution in place of another." />

<OrientationNote>
  <p>
    Measure notation keeps support mismatch and the density ratio
    $dQ/dP$ explicit. The finite-sum and density formulas are special cases.
  </p>
</OrientationNote>

<p>
The Kullback-Leibler divergence compares two probability measures on the same
measurable space. If $Q$ is absolutely continuous with respect to $P$, written
$Q\ll P$, then
</p>

$$
\mathrm&#123;KL&#125;[Q\Vert P]
= \int \log\frac&#123;dQ&#125;&#123;dP&#125;\,dQ
= \mathbb&#123;E&#125;_Q\!\left[\log\frac&#123;dQ&#125;&#123;dP&#125;\right].
$$

<p>
Equivalently, by changing the integrating measure back to $P$,
$\mathrm&#123;KL&#125;[Q\Vert P]=\int \frac&#123;dQ&#125;&#123;dP&#125;\log\frac&#123;dQ&#125;&#123;dP&#125;\,dP$. When both measures
have densities $q$ and $p$ with respect to a common base measure, this reduces to the
familiar formula $\int q(x)\log(q(x)/p(x))\,dx$.
These are Lebesgue integrals; the compact form integrates $\log(dQ/dP)$ with respect
to the probability measure $Q$.
</p>

<p>
KL is an expectation under the left-hand measure: samples are drawn from $Q$,
and each sample charges the log Radon-Nikodym derivative between what $Q$ expects and
what $P$ assigned. That is why KL is directed. The distribution on the left decides
where the comparison spends its mass.
</p>

<Aside>
  <strong>One sentence:</strong> $\mathrm&#123;KL&#125;[q\Vert p]$ is the average extra log-cost
  of pretending the data-generating distribution is $p$ when it is actually $q$.
</Aside>

<h2>1. The Radon-Nikodym derivative inside KL</h2>

<p>
The figure below keeps the base space ordinary, so $dQ/dP$ is just the density ratio
$q(x)/p(x)$. The top panel shows the two probability densities, the middle panel
shows the Radon-Nikodym derivative, and the bottom panel shows the KL integrand
$q(x)\log(q(x)/p(x))$. In the fully measure-theoretic formula, the middle curve is
the object being logged.
</p>

<Figure title={String.raw`Figure 1 · $\mathrm{KL}[Q\Vert P] = \int \log(dQ/dP)\,dQ$`} canvasId="fig-rn" width="760" height="420" readoutId="fig-rn-readout">
<Fragment slot="legend">
  <div class="legend">
      <span><span class="swatch" style="background:#b8412a"></span>$Q$ / $q(x)$</span>
      <span><span class="swatch" style="background:#1f4a8c"></span>$P$ / $p(x)$</span>
      <span><span class="swatch" style="background:#2d7a3e"></span>$dQ/dP = q/p$</span>
      <span><span class="swatch" style="background:rgba(107,69,146,0.55)"></span>$q\log(q/p)$</span>
    </div>
</Fragment>
<Fragment slot="controls">
  <ControlRange id="fig-rn-mq" label={String.raw`$\mu_Q$`} valueId="fig-rn-mq-v" min="-3" max="3" step="0.05" value="0.8" />
      <ControlRange id="fig-rn-sq" label={String.raw`$\sigma_Q$`} valueId="fig-rn-sq-v" min="0.3" max="3" step="0.05" value="1.1" />
      <PresetRow>
        <button data-fig-rn-preset="match">Match</button>
        <button data-fig-rn-preset="shift">Mean shift</button>
        <button data-fig-rn-preset="wide">Wide $Q$</button>
        <button data-fig-rn-preset="narrow">Narrow $Q$</button>
      </PresetRow>
</Fragment>
</Figure>

<p>
If $Q\not\ll P$, the derivative $dQ/dP$ does not exist as a finite density on all the
places $Q$ needs it, and $\mathrm&#123;KL&#125;[Q\Vert P]=+\infty$. This is the
measure-theoretic version of the discrete rule that putting positive $q_i$ where
$p_i=0$ gives infinite KL.
</p>

<NotationAside label="Density form">
  <p>
    For laws with densities on $\mathbb R$ (Lebesgue) or on a countable space
    (counting), the same definition reads
    $\mathrm&#123;KL&#125;[Q \Vert P] = \int q(x)\log(q(x)/p(x))\,dx$ or
    $\sum_i q_i\log(q_i/p_i)$. The absolute-continuity condition $Q \ll P$
    becomes "$q_i = 0$ wherever $p_i = 0$" — division-by-zero in the density
    form is the structural condition in the measure-theoretic form. See the
    <a href="/math/notation/#kl">notation page</a>.
  </p>
</NotationAside>

<h2>2. KL direction sets the averaging measure</h2>

<p>
KL is zero only when the two distributions match, but it is not a distance. Usually
$\mathrm&#123;KL&#125;[q\Vert p] \neq \mathrm&#123;KL&#125;[p\Vert q]$. The figure below uses Gaussians
because the exact value is available in closed form, while the lower plot shows the
pointwise contribution $q(x)\log(q(x)/p(x))$.
</p>

